TextBox Control: Used to receive keyboard input from the user.
Text Property: Stores the input from a TextBox as a string.
Variable: A storage location in memory used to hold data.
Variable Declaration: Variables must be declared with a data type before use.
Data Type: Defines the type of data a variable can store.
Variable Names: Should be meaningful and follow C# naming rules.
String: Stores a sequence of characters.
String Concatenation: Combines strings using the + operator.
Scope: Defines where a variable can be accessed.
Local Variable: A variable declared inside a method.
Variable Lifetime: The period during which a variable exists in memory.
Assignment Compatibility: Values assigned to variables must be compatible with their data types.
Initialization: Giving a variable a value before using it.
Numeric Data Types: int, double, and decimal are used for numeric data.
Numeric Literal: A number written directly in the program.
Type Casting: Explicitly converting one numeric data type to another.
var Keyword: Allows the compiler to determine a variable's type from its initial value.
Arithmetic Operators: +, -, *, /, and % perform calculations.
Order of Operations: Determines the sequence in which mathematical expressions are evaluated.
Integer Division: Division between two integers produces an integer result.
Numeric Input: TextBox input is received as a string even when it represents a number.
Parse Methods: Convert strings into numeric values, such as int.Parse() and double.Parse().
ToString() Method: Converts numeric values into strings for display